Rio
===

Rio's own code is licensed under the GNU General Public License v3.0 only (LICENSE).

Rio is built on Chromium, the open source browser project, and ships Chromium's code under Chromium's licenses (BSD-3-Clause
for the project itself, plus the licenses of the third-party components listed below).

This repository holds Rio's own code and a set of patches against Chromium 158. It does not contain the Chromium tree.
The Chromium checkout that apply.sh patches (see README.md) carries the full license texts: LICENSE at the top of that
checkout and a LICENSE or README.chromium file in each third_party directory.

A built Rio.app lists every Chromium and third-party component with its license text on its credits page, about:credits.
That page is generated from the Chromium checkout when the app is built. Redistribute it with any binary you ship.

Third-party code that Rio adds outside Chromium:

- EasyList and EasyPrivacy (https://easylist.to/), fetched at build time for the ad blocker and used under CC BY-SA 3.0.
  Credit: the EasyList authors. See chromium/clu/third_party/easylist.
- Sparkle 2 (https://sparkle-project.org/), downloaded by scripts/chromium/fetch-sparkle.sh at the version and checksum
  pinned in chromium/sparkle.pin, and packaged into release builds for updates. Sparkle is under its own license
  (MIT with bundled components); its license file ships in the app bundle.
- npm packages for the UI build and tests, each under its own license (package.json, package-lock.json).

